## Section XIV

OTHER MEASURES OF CRIMINAL-LEGAL CHARACTER
{Title of Section XIV as worded by Law No. 222-VII of 18.04.2013}

### Article 92. Concept and purpose of compulsory measures of medical character

Compulsory measures of medical character are provision of outpatient psychiatric care, placement of a person who has committed a socially dangerous act falling under the characteristics of an act provided for by the Special Part of this Code in a special medical institution for the purpose of their mandatory treatment, as well as prevention of commission by them of socially dangerous acts.

### Article 93. Persons to whom compulsory measures of medical character are applied

Compulsory measures of medical character may be applied by a court to persons:
1) who have committed socially dangerous acts in a state of insanity;
2) who have committed criminal offenses in a state of diminished sanity;
3) who have committed a criminal offense in a state of sanity but fell ill with mental illness before pronouncement of sentence or during serving of punishment.
{Article 93 as amended pursuant to Law No. 2617-VIII of 22.11.2018}

### Article 94. Types of compulsory measures of medical character

1. Depending on the nature and severity of the illness, severity of the act committed, taking into account the degree of danger of the mentally ill person to themselves or other persons, the court may apply the following compulsory measures of medical character:
1) provision of outpatient psychiatric care on a compulsory basis;
2) hospitalization to an institution providing psychiatric care with ordinary supervision;
3) hospitalization to an institution providing psychiatric care with enhanced supervision;
4) hospitalization to an institution providing psychiatric care with strict supervision.
2. Provision of outpatient psychiatric care on a compulsory basis may be applied by a court in respect of a person suffering from mental disorders and who has committed a socially dangerous act, if the person by the state of their mental health does not require hospitalization to an institution providing psychiatric care.
3. Hospitalization to an institution providing psychiatric care with ordinary supervision may be applied by a court in respect of a mentally ill person who by their mental state and the nature of the socially dangerous act committed requires detention in an institution providing psychiatric care and treatment on a compulsory basis.
4. Hospitalization to an institution providing psychiatric care with enhanced supervision may be applied by a court in respect of a mentally ill person who has committed a socially dangerous act not related to encroachment on the life of other persons, and by their mental state does not pose a threat to society, but requires detention in an institution providing psychiatric care and treatment under conditions of enhanced supervision.
5. Hospitalization to an institution providing psychiatric care with strict supervision may be applied by a court in respect of a mentally ill person who has committed a socially dangerous act related to encroachment on the life of other persons, as well as in respect of a mentally ill person who by their mental state and the nature of the socially dangerous act committed poses a particular danger to society and requires detention in an institution providing psychiatric care and treatment under conditions of strict supervision.
6. If it is not deemed necessary to apply compulsory measures of medical character to a mentally ill person, as well as in case of termination of application of such measures, the court may transfer them to the care of relatives or guardians with mandatory medical supervision.

